items/def-qftx-principal-connection-form.md:106:1.1 Write $p=s(x)a$ in an equivariant principal chart, with $s$ its local section and $a\in G$. Then $\phi_p(h)=p h=s(x)(a h)$; freeness and transitivity give bijectivity on the fibre, and this coordinate formula gives a smooth inverse. Hence $\phi_p:G\to P_x$, $h\mapsto p h$, is a diffeomorphism, so its differential at $e$ is a linear isomorphism $T_eG\to T_pP_x=V_pP$. The curve $t\mapsto p\exp_G(tZ)$ is $\phi_p\circ\exp_G(tZ)$, so its derivative is exactly $d(\phi_p)_eZ$; hence $\zeta_p$ is the stated isomorphism. [A1, F1, F3, F4, F6]
items/def-qftx-principal-connection-form.md:108:2.1 In the same chart, $\zeta_p$ is the derivative of the smooth action map and its inverse is the derivative of the inverse fibre coordinate, so the family $\zeta$ and its inverse are smooth bundle maps. The complement $TP=VP\oplus H$ gives a smooth bundle projection $v$, so $\omega=\zeta^{-1}\circ v$ is a smooth fibrewise-linear 1-form. On a vertical vector $Z_P(p)=\zeta_p(Z)$, the projection is the vector itself; therefore $\omega(Z_P)=Z$. Also $v_pX=0$ exactly when $X\in H_p$, so $\ker\omega=H$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1]
items/def-qftx-principal-connection-form.md:110:2.2 For $g\in G$, let $R_g(p)=pg$. The maps $R_g\circ\phi_p$ and $\phi_{pg}\circ C_{g^{-1}}$ agree because $p h g=pg(g^{-1}hg)$. Differentiating at $e$ by [F6] gives $d(R_g)_p Z_P(p)=(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Z)_P(pg)$. Since $H$ and $VP$ are right-invariant, their projections commute with $dR_g$; applying the definition of $\omega$ yields $\omega_{pg}(dR_gX)=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega_p(X)$. [F1, F2, F5, F6, step 1.1]
items/def-qftx-principal-connection-form.md:112:3.1 Conversely, if $\omega$ has the stated reproduction and equivariance properties, define $v:=\zeta\circ\omega$ and $H:=\ker\omega$. Reproduction gives $v\circ\zeta=\zeta$, hence $v^2=v$, $\operatorname{im}v=VP$, and $TP=VP\oplus H$. Equivariance of $\omega$ and the orbit differential identity from step 2.2 make $H$ right-invariant; smoothness of $\omega$ makes it a smooth subbundle. Thus this is a principal connection and the construction is inverse to step 2.1. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2]
items/def-qftx-principal-connection-form.md:114:4.1 The imported curvature $K_H$ is a smooth alternating map with values in $VP$, and $\omega$ restricts to the inverse of $\zeta:P\times\mathfrak g\to VP$; consequently $\kappa_H=\omega\circ K_H$ is a well-defined smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued alternating 2-form. This is only the conversion of its target fibre; the Cartan identity is proved separately. [F2, step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎
