+
+## Example
+
+Use natural units $\hbar=c=1$ and $M=\mathbb R^4$ with its standard orientation and Euclidean metric. The selected field model is pure compact Maxwell theory with actual gauge group $U(1)=\{e^{i\alpha}:\alpha\in\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z\}$, no dynamical electric or magnetic matter, and a smooth principal $U(1)$-bundle with connection on the complement of admitted external line insertions. Identify $\mathfrak u(1)=i\mathbb R$ with $\mathbb R$ so local potentials $A_i$ have holonomy $\exp(i\int A_i)$ and the real curvature $F$ obeys $F|_{U_i}=dA_i$. The model fixes the action convention $S[A]=(2e^2)^{-1}\int F\wedge *F$ with dimensionless $e>0$, the allowed bundle sectors, and any regulator or measure; no continuum measure is constructed here. With this normalization $[A_i]=\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $[F]=\mathrm{length}^{-2}$, and $\int_\Sigma F$ is dimensionless.
+
+Let $C$ be a closed smooth oriented curve carrying an admitted genuine external magnetic line $T_m(C)$, where genuine means that no auxiliary surface is attached to define the insertion. Its integer charge $m\in\mathbb Z$ is specified by $\int_{S^2_{\mathrm{link}}}F=2\pi m$ on every sufficiently small oriented normal two-sphere linking $C$. The model has no other magnetic sources, and its normalized correlation functional is assumed to be defined for these insertions.
+
+For a closed smooth oriented surface $\Sigma$ disjoint from the line, and $\alpha\in\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z$, define the magnetic surface operator
+
+$$
+U_\alpha(\Sigma)=\exp\!\left(\frac{i\alpha}{2\pi}\int_\Sigma F\right).
+$$
+
+If $\Sigma_0$ and $\Sigma_1$ are joined by a compact smooth singular three-chain $V$ whose boundary is $\Sigma_1-\Sigma_0$, transverse to $C$, write $I(V,C)$ for the signed number of intersections of $V$ with $C$. The claimed action is that a source-free move leaves $U_\alpha$ unchanged, while a move across the line multiplies it by $e^{i\alpha m I(V,C)}$. In particular, the line carries the character $\chi_m(e^{i\alpha})=e^{i\alpha m}$ of the magnetic one-form $U(1)$ symmetry.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Gaiotto, Kapustin, Seiberg, and Willett, §4.1, PDF pp. 14–16](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.5148)
+
+[Bhardwaj et al., §2.2.2 Example 2.3 and §4.1 Example 4.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.07547)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The compact $U(1)$ field model, its connection and flux normalization, the external line boundary condition, and the finite-chain support motion in the Example.
+
+[A1] The selected model has $\mathfrak u(1)=i\mathbb R$, no dynamical magnetic sources, and a genuine external line with integer flux $2\pi m$ through every small linking $S^2$. It specifies a correlation functional for the listed insertions; this is model input, not an experimental premise.
+
+[A2] The mathematical connection and Bianchi inputs require only $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, not full Choice. [[def-countable-choice]]
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]]: for a smooth right principal bundle and supplied connection, $\omega$ is a global Lie-algebra-valued one-form; the right-action and local-section conventions are fixed.
+
+[F2] [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: with $\Omega_\omega=d\omega+\frac12[\omega\wedge\omega]$ and local $F_i=s_i^*\Omega_\omega$, one has $D_{A_i}F_i=dF_i+[A_i\wedge F_i]=0$ and $F_i=dA_i+\frac12[A_i\wedge A_i]$.
+
+[F3] [[thm-stokes-theorem-for-smooth-singular-chains]]: for a smooth manifold, smooth form $\eta$, and finite smooth singular chain $V$ of degree $k\ge1$, $\int_V d\eta=\int_{\partial V}\eta$.
+
+[F4] [[def-aqft-p-form-global-symmetry]]: a magnetic one-form symmetry surface has codimension two and its charge is read from the crossing action on a genuine line operator.
+
+[F5] [[pthm-aqft-p-form-charge-characters]]: under its unitary, exchange, and simplicity hypotheses, the charge of a simple line is a continuous character of the Abelian one-form symmetry group.
+
+## Verification
+
+**Given:** [A1], [A2], and [F1]–[F5].
+
+1.1 The Lie algebra of $U(1)$ is Abelian, so $[A_i\wedge F_i]=0$. Applying [F2] gives $dF_i=0$ in every chart away from the external line; the forms $F_i$ patch to the global curvature $F$. [A1, A2, F1, F2, algebra]
+
+1.2 Suppose the swept chain $V$ is disjoint from $C$. By [F3] and step 1.1, $\int_{\Sigma_1}F-\int_{\Sigma_0}F=\int_{\partial V}F=\int_VdF=0$. The defining exponential is therefore unchanged by every admitted source-free isotopy, so the surface operator is topological in this sector. [A1, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 If $V$ meets $C$ transversely, remove small open three-balls around its finitely many intersection points. The remaining chain lies in the source-free region; its boundary is $\Sigma_1-\Sigma_0-\sum_j\epsilon_j S_j^2$, where $\epsilon_j\in\{+1,-1\}$ is the local oriented intersection sign and $S_j^2$ is the small linking sphere. Applying [F3] to the punctured chain and using step 1.1 gives $\int_{\Sigma_1}F-\int_{\Sigma_0}F=\sum_j\epsilon_j\int_{S_j^2}F=2\pi m I(V,C)$ by [A1]. [A1, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.2 Substitution into the definition of $U_\alpha$ yields $U_\alpha(\Sigma_1)T_m(C)=e^{i\alpha m I(V,C)}T_m(C)U_\alpha(\Sigma_0)$. For one positively oriented linking, the phase is $e^{i\alpha m}$. [A1, F4, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+2.3 Since $m\in\mathbb Z$, $\chi_m(e^{i(\alpha+2\pi)})=\chi_m(e^{i\alpha})$; also $\chi_m(e^{i(\alpha+\beta)})=\chi_m(e^{i\alpha})\chi_m(e^{i\beta})$ and $\chi_m(1)=1$. Thus $\chi_m$ is a continuous character, and [F5] identifies it as the line's one-form charge. [A1, F5, step 2.2, algebra]
+
+3.1 If $m=0$ or $\alpha=0$, the linking phase is one. Reversing the orientation of the line changes $m$ to $-m$, and reversing the swept-chain orientation changes $I$ to $-I$, so the formula respects the declared orientation convention. No equation of motion, observation, or continuum-existence conclusion is used. [A1, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra] $\square$
+
