+## Definition
+
+Fix an integer $d\ge1$, an integer $p$ with $0\le p<d$, and a smooth $d$-dimensional spacetime manifold $M$, possibly with boundary. A selected model $\mathcal T$ specifies its spacetime structure and boundary conditions; field configuration space, field bundles, regularity and admitted sectors; observables and their supports; normalized correlation functionals; and, when present, its action, regulator, measure, unit system and charge normalization. No such data are inferred from a familiar theory name.
+
+Use the preceding topological-interface definition with its exact content: an endodefect has the same complete model data on both sides, and it is an invertible symmetry interface only when it has a two-sided inverse under the model's fusion operation and induces an automorphism preserving the declared observables, backgrounds and correlators. A defect support is the image of a smooth embedding $\iota:\Sigma\hookrightarrow M^\circ$ from a manifold $\Sigma$ without boundary, with closed image in the interior $M^\circ$. Its model-supplied field, transmission, orientation, framing and junction data are part of the insertion. It is topological when all declared correlators are invariant under every admitted isotopy that avoids the other insertions and preserves boundary data.
+
+A **group-like $p$-form global symmetry** of $\mathcal T$ consists of a locally compact Hausdorff topological group $G$ and an assignment $g\mapsto U_g(\Sigma)$ of invertible symmetry interfaces to the allowed closed supports $\Sigma$ of dimension $d-p-1$ and codimension $p+1$. The model must define the parallel fusion operation and satisfy $U_e(\Sigma)=\mathbf 1_{\mathcal T}$ and $U_g(\Sigma)\star U_h(\Sigma)=U_{gh}(\Sigma)$ whenever that fusion is defined; $U_{g^{-1}}$ is its inverse. The group $G$ is **effective**: elements inducing identical actions in every admitted correlator and on every admitted observable and background are identified. A group element labels a transformation, not the charge of an observable.
+
+A **genuine $p$-dimensional observable** is an observable supported on a closed embedded $p$-dimensional submanifold whose definition is independent of an attached higher-dimensional support. Its symmetry action is defined by moving a symmetry interface across it, with the model specifying the resulting junction or operator action. For $p=0$, local observables may carry higher-dimensional representations of a non-Abelian group; the representation label is distinct from the group element implementing a transformation. For a locally compact Hausdorff Abelian group $A$, write its character group as $\widehat A=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{cts}}(A,U(1))$, with pointwise multiplication. Here $U(1)=\{e^{i\alpha}:\alpha\in\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z\}$, so every character value and its argument are dimensionless.
+
+A **background** for a symmetry is nondynamical model data, not summed over. For a finite Abelian group $A$, fix a triangulation $K$ of $M$ and let $C^k(K;A)$ be the group of functions from oriented $k$-simplices to $A$, with orientation reversal acting by negation. Its coboundary is the alternating sum over faces,
+
+$$
+(\delta c)([v_0,\ldots,v_{k+1}])=\sum_{j=0}^{k+1}(-1)^j c([v_0,\ldots,\widehat v_j,\ldots,v_{k+1}]).
+$$
+
+Since $\delta^2=0$ by pairwise cancellation of twice-deleted faces, a discrete $(p+1)$-form background with no higher-group mixing is a cocycle $B\in C^{p+1}(K;A)$ modulo $B\mapsto B+\delta\Lambda$ for $\Lambda\in C^p(K;A)$. Relative cochains or boundary-fixed gauge transformations are used when required by the model's boundary conditions. If other backgrounds mix with $B$, the model must give the modified cocycle and gauge-transformation laws.
+
+For a continuous $U(1)^r$ $p$-form symmetry, a background consists of model-supplied local $(p+1)$-form potentials, their overlap and lower-overlap transition data, a globally specified curvature locally given by $H=dB$, and the small and large gauge transformations with their holonomy and integral-flux normalization. Small transformations have the local form $B\mapsto B+d\Lambda$ for a $p$-form $\Lambda$. The unit system and all coefficients in background couplings remain model inputs. The model must define its correlators in these backgrounds; this definition asserts neither that every symmetry admits such a coupling nor that the symmetry can be gauged.
