      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The compact $U(1)$ field model, its connection and flux normalization, the external line boundary condition, and the finite-chain support motion in the Example.\n\n[A1] The selected model has $\\mathfrak u(1)=i\\mathbb R$, no dynamical magnetic sources, and a genuine external line with integer flux $2\\pi m$ through every small linking $S^2$. It specifies a correlation functional for the listed insertions; this is model input, not an experimental premise.\n\n[A2] The mathematical connection and Bianchi inputs require only $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, not full Choice. [[def-countable-choice]]\n\n[F1] [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]]: for a smooth right principal bundle and supplied connection, $\\omega$ is a global Lie-algebra-valued one-form; the right-action and local-section conventions are fixed.\n\n[F2] [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: with $\\Omega_\\omega=d\\omega+\\frac12[\\omega\\wedge\\omega]$ and local $F_i=s_i^*\\Omega_\\omega$, one has $D_{A_i}F_i=dF_i+[A_i\\wedge F_i]=0$ and $F_i=dA_i+\\frac12[A_i\\wedge A_i]$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-stokes-theorem-for-smooth-singular-chains]]: for a smooth manifold, smooth form $\\eta$, and finite smooth singular chain $V$ of degree $k\\ge1$, $\\int_V d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial V}\\eta$.\n\n[F4] [[def-aqft-p-form-global-symmetry]]: a magnetic one-form symmetry surface has codimension two and its charge is read from the crossing action on a genuine line operator.\n\n[F5] [[pthm-aqft-p-form-charge-characters]]: under its unitary, exchange, and simplicity hypotheses, the charge of a simple line is a continuous character of the Abelian one-form symmetry group.\n\n\n\n**Given:** [A1], [A2], and [F1]–[F5].\n\n1.1 The Lie algebra of $U(1)$ is Abelian, so $[A_i\\wedge F_i]=0$; its adjoint action is trivial, so the local forms $F_i$ agree on overlaps. Applying [F2] gives the global curvature $F$ and $dF_i=0$ in every chart away from the external line, hence $dF=0$ there. [A1, A2, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Suppose the swept chain $V$ is disjoint from $C$. By [F3] and step 1.1, $\\int_{\\Sigma_1}F-\\int_{\\Sigma_0}F=\\int_{\\partial V}F=\\int_VdF=0$. The defining exponential is therefore unchanged by every admitted source-free isotopy, so the surface operator is topological in this sector. [A1, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 For a move past the line, the supplied chain lies in the source-free region and has $\\partial V=\\Sigma_1-\\Sigma_0-\\sum_j\\epsilon_jS_j^2$ by the Example's support convention. Applying [F3] and step 1.1 gives $0=\\int_VdF=\\int_{\\Sigma_1}F-\\int_{\\Sigma_0}F-\\sum_j\\epsilon_j\\int_{S_j^2}F$. The flux boundary condition in [A1] therefore gives $\\int_{\\Sigma_1}F-\\int_{\\Sigma_0}F=2\\pi m I(V,C)$. [A1, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Substitution into the definition of $U_\\alpha$ yields $U_\\alpha(\\Sigma_1)T_m(C)=e^{i\\alpha m I(V,C)}T_m(C)U_\\alpha(\\Sigma_0)$. For one positively oriented linking, the phase is $e^{i\\alpha m}$. [A1, F4, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 Since $m\\in\\mathbb Z$, $\\chi_m(e^{i(\\alpha+2\\pi)})=\\chi_m(e^{i\\alpha})$; also $\\chi_m(e^{i(\\alpha+\\beta)})=\\chi_m(e^{i\\alpha})\\chi_m(e^{i\\beta})$ and $\\chi_m(1)=1$. Thus $\\chi_m$ is a continuous character, and [F5] identifies it as the line's one-form charge. [A1, F5, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 If $m=0$ or $\\alpha=0$, the linking phase is one. Reversing the orientation of the line changes $m$ to $-m$, and reversing the swept-chain orientation changes $I$ to $-I$, so the formula respects the declared orientation convention. No equation of motion, observation, or continuum-existence conclusion is used. [A1, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎"
    },
    {
