        "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$, an integer $p$ with $0\\le p<d$, and a smooth $d$-dimensional spacetime manifold $M$, possibly with boundary. A selected model $\\mathcal T$ specifies its spacetime structure and boundary conditions; field configuration space, field bundles, regularity and admitted sectors; observables and their supports; normalized correlation functionals; and, when present, its action, regulator, measure, unit system and charge normalization. No such data are inferred from a familiar theory name.\n\nUse the preceding topological-interface definition with its exact content: an endodefect has the same complete model data on both sides, and it is an invertible symmetry interface only when it has a two-sided inverse under the model's fusion operation and induces an automorphism preserving the declared observables, backgrounds and correlators. A defect support is the image of a smooth embedding $\\iota:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M^\\circ$ from a manifold $\\Sigma$ without boundary, with closed image in the interior $M^\\circ$. Its model-supplied field, transmission, orientation, framing and junction data are part of the insertion. It is topological when all declared correlators are invariant under every admitted isotopy that avoids the other insertions and preserves boundary data.\n\nA **group-like $p$-form global symmetry** of $\\mathcal T$ consists of a locally compact Hausdorff topological group $G$ and an assignment $g\\mapsto U_g(\\Sigma)$ of invertible symmetry interfaces to the allowed closed supports $\\Sigma$ of dimension $d-p-1$ and codimension $p+1$. The model must define the parallel fusion operation and satisfy $U_e(\\Sigma)=\\mathbf 1_{\\mathcal T}$ and $U_g(\\Sigma)\\star U_h(\\Sigma)=U_{gh}(\\Sigma)$ whenever that fusion is defined; $U_{g^{-1}}$ is its inverse. The group $G$ is **effective**: elements inducing identical actions in every admitted correlator and on every admitted observable and background are identified. A group