        "statement": "Let $d\\ge2$, $1\\le p<d$, and let $\\mathcal T$ have a group-like $p$-form global symmetry $G$ as defined above. Assume the selected model has a unitary Hilbert-space realization and, for every $g,h\\in G$, admits two parallel copies of their codimension-$(p+1)$ symmetry supports inside a contractible neighborhood with a disk spanned by two independent normal directions, together with an isotopy exchanging their order without intersecting any other insertion or changing boundary data. Then $G$ is Abelian.\n\nLet $O(C)$ be a nonzero genuine $p$-dimensional operator bounded in the operator norm of the selected Hilbert-space representation. Call it **simple for this result** when each topological local junction endomorphism on $O$ produced by passing a symmetry interface across it is a scalar multiple of $O$. Assume the induced action on the one-dimensional space spanned by $O$ is continuous and unitary. There is a continuous character $\\chi_O:G\\to U(1)$ such that passing $U_g$ across $O$ multiplies it by $\\chi_O(g)$. Thus the charge of $O$ belongs to the Pontryagin dual $\\widehat G=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G,U(1))$. This theorem makes no claim for $p=0$ or for operators with nonscalar crossing endomorphisms.",
